# gitleaks allowlist — reviewed exceptions, NOT a blanket rule disable.
#
# Each entry below is a `fingerprint` of the form:
#     <file>:<rule-id>:<line>
# which is the NARROWEST scope gitleaks supports: it suppresses exactly one
# rule at exactly one line of exactly one file. Adding a line to either file,
# or the same pattern appearing anywhere else in the repository, produces a
# NEW fingerprint and fails the gate. Nothing here weakens the scan globally.
#
# REVIEW DATE: 2026-02-28 (6 months). Re-read both lines at that point and
# delete any entry whose justification no longer holds.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. .env.example:19 — `JWT_SECRET=`
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The value is EMPTY. This is a documented local-development template whose own
# header instructs the reader to generate the value with `openssl rand -hex 32`,
# and .env itself is gitignored. gitleaks' `generic-api-key` rule matched the
# variable NAME, not a secret — there is no value present to leak.
#
# Rejected alternative: deleting the key from the template. That would remove
# the documentation of a variable the application REQUIRES to boot (it refuses
# to start without >=32 chars of HS256 key material), trading a false positive
# for a real onboarding failure.
#
.env.example:generic-api-key:19

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. scripts/smoke/jwt_check.sh:27 — `Authorization: Bearer forged.token.value`
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A deliberately INVALID token, and a negative-test fixture: step 2/4 of the
# smoke check asserts that a protected endpoint answers 401 to a forged
# credential. `forged.token.value` is not a JWT, has never been valid, and
# grants nothing. gitleaks' `curl-auth-header` rule matches the shape of the
# header, not the validity of the token.
#
# Rejected alternative: building the string at runtime to dodge the scanner.
# That hides the fixture from a human reading the test without changing what is
# sent, making the security assertion harder to review for no security gain.
#
# The REAL credentials in this script are never literals: they arrive as
# $SMOKE_USER / $SMOKE_PASSWORD and are JSON-escaped through python so they
# never appear on a command line or in the process table.
#
scripts/smoke/jwt_check.sh:curl-auth-header:27
